# ── ShopNest environment ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy to .env and adjust. NEVER commit real secrets to git.

# SQLite database (Prisma). Path is relative to the project root,
# or absolute for local sandboxes: file:/home/z/my-project/db/custom.db
DATABASE_URL=file:./db/custom.db

# Public origin of the site — REQUIRED for emailed links (password reset,
# email verification, order tracking) to work. No trailing slash.
#   Local dev:  http://localhost:3000 (fallback when unset)
#   Production: https://your-domain.com
#
# NOTE (build-time vs runtime): NEXT_PUBLIC_* variables are INLINED into the
# bundle at build time — a value set only at runtime is invisible to built
# server code. Either set NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL when you BUILD, or set the
# plain SITE_URL on the server at RUNTIME (it takes precedence).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=
# SITE_URL=https://your-domain.com

# Set when running behind a reverse proxy / CDN. Values:
#   TRUST_PROXY=1             behind YOUR OWN nginx/Caddy — trusts x-real-ip,
#                             then the LAST x-forwarded-for entry (the one
#                             YOUR proxy appended)
#   TRUST_PROXY=cf            behind Cloudflare — trusts cf-connecting-ip
#                             (Cloudflare overwrites client-supplied copies;
#                             with any other proxy this header is forgeable,
#                             so only use "cf" when Cloudflare is really in
#                             front)
# Leave unset when the server is exposed directly. Verified behaviour for
# direct exposure (Next 16 standalone): Next injects the socket address into
# x-forwarded-for when the client sends none, so honest clients are limited
# per-IP — but a client CAN forge a single-entry XFF to rotate buckets;
# per-identity limits (email, session) are the rotation-proof backstop.
# TRUST_PROXY=1

# Dedicated database file for rate-limit buckets. Separate from the main
# database on purpose: limiter writes never contend with checkout for the
# main database's write lock. Optional — defaults to db/ratelimit.db.
# RATELIMIT_DATABASE_URL=file:./db/ratelimit.db

# Optional: require a verified email before customers can write reviews.
# Only takes effect while a mail provider (RESEND_API_KEY) is configured —
# without one, verification is impossible and the gate stays off.
# REQUIRE_VERIFIED_EMAIL=0

# Optional: GUESTS must confirm their checkout email with a one-time code
# (round-4 anti-spam: without it anyone could order with someone else's
# email and the platform would send that person unsolicited confirmation
# mail). Same condition as the reviews gate — only while a mail provider is
# configured (without one no mail ever leaves the server, so there is
# nothing to abuse). Disable explicitly with:
# REQUIRE_GUEST_EMAIL_OTP=0

# Session signing secrets (customer + admin are separate by design).
# MUST be at least 32 random chars. Generate your own with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
SESSION_SECRET=
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=

# Admin bootstrap credentials for `bun prisma/seed.ts`.
# When ADMIN_SEED_PASSWORD is unset a random password is generated,
# printed once to the console, and the account is flagged
# mustChangePassword (forced reset on first login).
# ADMIN_SEED_EMAIL=admin@shopnest.com
# ADMIN_SEED_PASSWORD=

# Optional: customer email delivery via Resend (https://resend.com).
# Without a provider, mails are queued in the MailOutbox table with one-time
# tokens REDACTED (a DB/outbox leak must never enable account takeover) —
# so password reset and email verification REQUIRE a configured provider.
# In development the full link is printed to the server console instead.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# MAIL_FROM="ShopNest <no-reply@shopnest.com>"

# Dedicated encryption key for admin TOTP secrets (openssl rand -hex 32).
# MANDATORY IN PRODUCTION (round-4: the server refuses to boot without it) —
# otherwise the key is derived from ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET and rotating THAT
# secret invalidates every admin's 2FA. With it, only rotating TOTP_ENC_KEY
# re-enrolls 2FA (admins re-enable from Security → 2FA; recovery codes keep
# working — they are stored key-free).
# TOTP_ENC_KEY=

# Optional server-side pixel forwarding credentials are stored in the
# TrackingIntegration table (admin console → Settings → Tracking).
